COMMAND NAME: mppstop.sh

Stops a Greenplum Database database management server instance.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mppstop.sh [-d <data_directory>] [-n | -z] [-f | -i | -s] [-r] 
[-m] [-y] [-a] [-q] [-l <logfile_directory>]

mppstop.sh [-t | -? | -v]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The mppstop.sh script is analogous to functionality offered by the 
PostgreSQL pg_ctl and postmaster commands, which are used to stop the 
database server. When you stop a Greenplum Database database management server 
instance, you are actually stopping several postmaster processes at 
once (the MPP master and all of the segment instances). The mppstop.sh 
script handles the shutdown of the individual instances. Each instance 
is shutdown sequentially by default.

Before you can stop a Greenplum Database database server, you must have started 
it using mppstart.sh first.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-d <data_directory>

Optional. The master host data directory. If not specified, the value 
set for $MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.

-n

Optional. Shuts down the segment instances in parallel. In this mode, 
the shut down process does not wait for return codes from the segment 
instances. The default behavior is to shut down the segment instances 
sequentially and wait for their return codes.

-z

Optional. Forces the shutdown of a segment instance with an invalid status. 
This may be desirable if the segment instance or host had a minor error 
during startup, and you want to shut down the system to fix the error, 
then retry startup again. The default behavior is to not shutdown segment 
instances with an invalid status.

-f

Fast shut down. Any transactions in progress are interrupted and rolled back.

-i

Immediate shut down. Any transactions in progress are aborted. Use this 
option with care if you are running in continue fault tolerance mode 
(read-write), as this could cause data corruption.

-s

Smart shut down. Waits for active transactions to complete before shutting 
down. This is the default shutdown mode.

-r 

Restart after shutdown is complete.

-m

Optional. Shuts down a MPP master instance that was started in 
maintenance mode.

-y

Do not stop the standby master process. The default is to stop 
the standby master.

-a

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.

-q

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, 
but is still written to the log file.

-l <logfile_directory>

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/mppAdminLogs.

-t

Displays the settings that will be used to shut down the system, 
such as the master data directory and port, and the name, data directory, 
port, valid status, and primary or mirror status of all the segment instances.

-?

Displays the online help.

-v

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Stop a Greenplum Database database management server instance in smart mode:

mppstop.sh


Stop a Greenplum Database database management server instance in fast mode:

mppstop.sh -f


Force shutdown of the system if it has failed segment instances from a 
previous startup attempt:

mppstop.sh -z


Stop all segment instances and then restart the system:

mppstop.sh -r


Stop all segment instances in parallel (no return code status):

mppstop.sh -n


Stop a MPP master instance that was started in maintenance mode:

mppstop.sh -m


Display the online help for the mppstop.sh script:

mppstop.sh -?


